s":[{"name":"Indochina","confidence":0.85},{"name":"Polynesia","confidence":0.8},{"name":"Madagascar","confidence":0.8}],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Catholic missionaries and republican critics jointly shaped French colonial policies, perspectives, and domestic politics, challenging the view that French colonialism was defined by secular republican ideology alone.","confidence":0.95},"academicOrientation":{"score":72,"confidence":0.9},"profileConfidence":0.93,"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"317177b49ba9779621a780454d9cc6feb7512fd330c9e77b3449eae687694f68","reviewStatus":"flagged","validationWarnings":["central_places_hit_schema_cap"]},"primarySubject":"World History","secondarySubjects":["Religion","Politics & Government"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book explores the interplay of religion and politics within the context of French colonialism during a specific historical period, making World History the dominant subject.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"d45c0f418468d17c36d7c35199c4346b034423957a0aaa916dc432b52420b09e","summary":"Between 1880 and 1914, tens of thousands of men and women left France for distant religious missions, driven by the desire to spread the word of Jesus Christ, combat Satan, and convert the world's pagans to Catholicism. But they were not the only ones with eyes fixed on foreign shores. Just as the Catholic missionary movement reached its apex, the young, staunchly secular Third Republic launched the most aggressive campaign of colonial expansion in French history. Missionaries and republicans abroad knew they had much to gain from working together, but their starkly different motivations regularly led them to view one another with resentment, distrust, and even fear.In An Empire Divided, J.P. Daughton tells the story of how troubled relations between Catholic missionaries and a host of republican critics shaped colonial policies, Catholic perspectives, and domestic French politics in the tumultuous decades before the First World War. With case studies on Indochina, Polynesia, and Madagascar, An Empire Divided--the first book to examine the role of religious missionaries in shaping French colonialism--challenges the long-held view that French colonizing and \"civilizing\" goals were shaped by a distinctly secular republican ideology built on Enlightenment ideals.","pageCount":344,"publicationYear":2006,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","label":"History","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-an-empire-divided-religion-republicanism-and-the-making-of-french-colonialism-1880-1914-j-p-daug"}],"displaySummary":"With case studies on Indochina, Polynesia, and Madagascar, An Empire Divided--the first book to examine the role of religious missionaries in shaping French colonialism--challenges the long-held view that French colonizing and \"civilizing\" goals were shaped by a distinctly secular republican ideology built on Enlightenment ideals.","primaryTopic":"World War I","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"World History","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"World History","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"World History","mappedSubject":"World History","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book explores the interplay of religion and politics within the context of French colonialism during a specific historical period, making World History the dominant subject.","id":"subject-evidence-book-an-empire-divided-religion-republicanism-and-the-making-of-french-colonialism-1880-1914-j-p-daug-1"},{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","rawLabel":"History","mappedSubject":"History","score":3,"confidence":"medium","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-an-empire-divided-religion-republicanism-and-the-making-of-french-colonialism-1880-1914-j-p-daug","id":"subject-evidence-book-an-emp